WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.418
Encryption and data protection controls should be selected based on the security property needed and the workflow.

2
00:00:07.418 --> 00:00:16.274
Data in transit, recoverable stored data, framework payloads, passwords, and application secrets all have different protection needs.

3
00:00:16.274 --> 00:00:20.059
One control should not be stretched to solve every problem.

4
00:00:20.059 --> 00:00:25.433
Use TLS for data in transit rather than trying to build custom transport protection.

5
00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:37.242
Use ASP. NET Core Data Protection for appropriate protected framework and application payloads such as cookies, antiforgery tokens, reset tokens, and short-lived protected values.

6
00:00:37.242 --> 00:00:45.038
Use maintained cryptography APIs and platform services instead of custom algorithms or copied cryptographic snippets.

7
00:00:45.038 --> 00:00:47.007
Passwords are a separate case.

8
00:00:47.007 --> 00:00:51.397
Passwords should use password hashing, not reversible encryption.

9
00:00:51.397 --> 00:00:58.966
A password hashing design verifies the submitted password without storing a value that is meant to be recovered later.

10
00:00:58.966 --> 00:01:05.098
That is a different goal from protecting a value that the application must decrypt or unprotect.

11
00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:13.348
Field-level encryption, database encryption, disk encryption, and application-level protection solve different problems.

12
00:01:13.348 --> 00:01:17.587
Database or disk encryption may protect storage media or backups.

13
00:01:17.587 --> 00:01:21.675
Field-level encryption may limit exposure of selected values.

14
00:01:21.675 --> 00:01:26.519
Application-level protection may control who can recover a value and when.

15
00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:31.969
Each choice affects search, indexing, rotation, backup, recovery, and operations.

16
00:01:31.969 --> 00:01:40.826
Key management matters. Keys need secure generation, storage, access control, rotation, revocation, backup, and compromise response.

17
00:01:40.826 --> 00:01:53.012
The long-term strength of encryption depends not only on the algorithm, but on who can access the keys, how keys are monitored, and how the organization responds when key material must change.
